  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 312 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nWe're keeping an eye on another storm system progged to arrive at\nthe start of the Long Term period. Models show an upper level \nshortwave digging into the Desert Southwest Monday, then reaching \nour area sometime Monday night. This sends a reinforcing shot of \ncold air down across our region, dropping Monday night's lows into\nthe 30s, with 40s along the Rio Grande. Lift and moisture \nassociated with this system also brings forth increasing \nprecipitation chances. At the moment, PoPs are low (10-30%) across\nmuch of the region Monday evening, generally favoring SE NM. \nEarly Tuesday morning, low (10-30%) PoPs shift toward the eastern\nhalf of the region, with the best odds favoring our easternmost \ncounties. Given current forecast temperature profiles, precip \nlooks to be in the form of light rain to even a few snow flurries \nMonday night into Tuesday morning. The best chances for snow \nflurries would be over SE NM and the northern half of the Permian \nBasin. There remains uncertainty regarding exact timing and \nlocation of any snow showers that develop, so stay tuned for \nupdates! Otherwise, little to no wintry accumulation is expected \nprovided warm ground temperatures and Tuesday's highs reaching the\nmid 40s to mid 50s. Tuesday night will be the coldest in the \nforecast period. Lows shall slip into the upper 20s in our \nnorthern counties and the higher terrain, and 30s almost \neverywhere else. \n\nThe aforementioned shortwave departs to our east Wednesday, giving \nway to an upper ridge. Highs in the 50s become more prevalent \nWednesday afternoon and Thanksgiving Day. Models depict another \nstorm system taking shape heading into the upcoming weekend. \nConfidence is low regarding the track and strength of this \npotential upper low, but we'll continue to monitor trends as the \nweek progresses. For now, there's a slight signal for this system \nto bring us more rain chances. Temperatures also look to continue \na warming trend over the weekend. Keep posted!",
